{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n 'unlock|return|setMood|setSfx|setMusic' audio.js | tail -35; sed -n '112,146p' hub/README.md; rg -n 'isServer|server:|me=' hub/session.js | head -12; cat test-results.json\"","aggregated_output":"5:  let unlocked = false, musicVolume = 0.1, effectsVolume = 0.72;\n9:  const rand = () => { randomSeed = (1664525 * randomSeed + 1013904223) >>> 0; return randomSeed / 4294967296; };\n14:    if (master) return;\n96:    if (!unlocked || !ctx || (ctx.state === 'suspended' && !/Offline/.test(ctx.constructor.name))) return;\n117:    if (!unlocked || !effectsBus) return;\n144:    if (!unlocked || !ctx || ctx.currentTime < nextVoice) return;\n168:  return {\n169:    async unlock() {\n170:      try { build(); if (ctx.state === 'suspended' && !/Offline/.test(ctx.constructor.name)) await ctx.resume(); unlocked = true; scheduleMusic(); return true; }\n171:      catch { return false; }\n173:    setMusic(value) { musicVolume = clamp(value); if (musicBus) musicBus.gain.setTargetAtTime(musicVolume, ctx.currentTime, 0.08); },\n174:    setSfx(value) { effectsVolume = clamp(value); if (effectsBus) effectsBus.gain.setTargetAtTime(effectsVolume, ctx.currentTime, 0.04); },\n175:    setMood(value) { if (['explore', 'sneak', 'flight', 'win'].includes(value) && mood !== value) { mood = value; step = 0; if (ctx) nextMusic = ctx.currentTime + 0.08; } },\n181:      return { unlocked, state: ctx?.state || 'locked', contextState: ctx?.state || 'locked', mood, musicVolume, effectsVolume, rms, peak, ...counts, sfxCount: counts.effects, voiceCount: counts.voice [... omitted end of long line]\n183:    dispose() { ambience?.stop(); if (ctx && !/Offline/.test(ctx.constructor.name)) ctx.close(); unlocked = false; }\n  round's `info`, or of `null` when there is no hub after all; with `null`\n  the game runs as it does on its own.\n- `AIHub.ready()` is called once, when the game has loaded and the round can\n  start, with no key press: the players answer the card.\n- The round starts when the card's countdown ends or every player pressed\n  Ready, and every game has called `ready()`. A game still loading then gets\n  20 more seconds; after that its player sits the round out.\n\n`info` (also on `AIHub` after `connect()`):\n\n| Field | |\n|---|---|\n| `me` | this copy's player id |\n| `players` | `[{ id, name, color }]`, ids `0..N-1` in seat order |\n| `host`, `isHost` | the id of the copy that runs a shared world, and whether it is this one |\n| `server` | `true` only on a copy that hosts a shared world with no player of its own |\n| `mode`, `round`, `limitMs` | the mode, the round number, and the round's hard limit after the start |\n| `seed` | a number, the same on every copy, behind `AIHub.rng(label)` |\n\nNames come from the hub and are never inserted into the page as HTML. Colours\nfollow the seat: `#e8443a`, `#3b82f6`, `#22c55e`, `#f5b700`.\n\n### The card\n\nBefore every round the players get 10 to 30 seconds to learn what comes\nnext and say when they are ready. A player can ask for more time, which adds\nten seconds; while more than half of them are asking, the countdown holds.\nBy default the hub shows its own card over the game. A game can make the\ncard its own, as part of the game:\n\n```js\nAIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card: true });\n```\n\nThe hub's card then steps aside when the game calls `ready()`, and the game's\n{\n  \"checks\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"correct game and ready\",\n      \"passed\": true\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"renderer initialized\",\n      \"passed\": true\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"title screen\",\n      \"passed\": true\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"uncaught browser errors\",\n      \"passed\": true\n    }\n  ],\n  \"browserErrors\": []\n}","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"agent_message","text":"The full game starts successfully with Three.js r180 and Rapier 0.19.3, and the visual check shows the ink-lined suburb and animated bicycle/visitor correctly. I’m tightening co-op action delivery and controller navigation now, then running the final playthrough and packaging the single-file build.\n"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"mv ../workspace.json workspace.json\ncat > README.md <<'EOF'\n# E.T. Moonward Bound\n\nA complete, three-act, 1–2 player neighborhood adventure inspired by E.T. the Extra-Terrestrial (1982).\n\nOpen the single HTML file beside this folder in a modern browser. It uses Three.js r180 WebGPURenderer and Rapier 0.19.3 from pinned CDNs. The first launch needs a network connection. WebGPU is preferred; the same renderer supplies WebGL 2 compatibility when WebGPU is unavailable.\n\nRide through the neighborhood to borrow three signal-machine parts. Avoid sweeping searchlights with slow movement or a friendship glow. Revive flowers and follow candy to recover friendship. At the forest clearing, echo the four-note light pattern. Then steer the bicycle through starlight rings, rise over search vans, and reach the visitor's ship.\n\n- Move: left stick / WASD or arrows; click ground to ride.\n- Interact: Cross / E.\n- Glow, or hold to lift during flight: Square / Space.\n- Sneak, or boost during flight: L2 / Shift.\n- Camera: right stick / drag the world.\n- Pause: Options / Escape. Developer playground is in Pause.\n- Signal: Square, Cross, Circle / 1, 2, 3; mouse and touch can choose lights directly.\n- Touch: invisible left thumbstick, right thumb camera, contextual action, assisted flight.\n\nBring a friend from the menu. Same-device rooms use a short neighborhood code. Direct online play exchanges an invitation and a reply using WebRTC; some networks may prevent direct connections. AI Hub connects its sessions automatically. Both players share progress, can retrieve parts, and can glow to protect their friend.\n\nThe title starts an AI-played attract adventure after ten seconds without input. Any input returns to the menu. A checkpoint is saved locally after finding parts and starting flight.\n\n## Development\n\nAll authored modules and validation artifacts are here. \"'`npm run build` makes a development `index.html` with local dependencies and the distributable HTML in the parent folder. `npm test` runs the unified headless checks; it prints only failures and one count. The test runner owns and closes its OS-selected localhost server and browser.\n\n`?webgl=1` forces the renderer'\"'s WebGL compatibility backend for visual inspection on headless machines whose WebGPU screenshots are blank. Normal play selects WebGPU automatically. \"'`window.__game` exposes state and deterministic gameplay controls for scripted QA.\n\nCredits and the machine-readable `exit-interview.json` beside the final HTML contain the requested source and testing reports.\nEOF'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"mv ../workspace.json workspace.json\ncat > README.md <<'EOF'\n# E.T. Moonward Bound\n\nA complete, three-act, 1–2 player neighborhood adventure inspired by E.T. the Extra-Terrestrial (1982).\n\nOpen the single HTML file beside this folder in a modern browser. It uses Three.js r180 WebGPURenderer and Rapier 0.19.3 from pinned CDNs. The first launch needs a network connection. WebGPU is preferred; the same renderer supplies WebGL 2 compatibility when WebGPU is unavailable.\n\nRide through the neighborhood to borrow three signal-machine parts. Avoid sweeping searchlights with slow movement or a friendship glow. Revive flowers and follow candy to recover friendship. At the forest clearing, echo the four-note light pattern. Then steer the bicycle through starlight rings, rise over search vans, and reach the visitor's ship.\n\n- Move: left stick / WASD or arrows; click ground to ride.\n- Interact: Cross / E.\n- Glow, or hold to lift during flight: Square / Space.\n- Sneak, or boost during flight: L2 / Shift.\n- Camera: right stick / drag the world.\n- Pause: Options / Escape. Developer playground is in Pause.\n- Signal: Square, Cross, Circle / 1, 2, 3; mouse and touch can choose lights directly.\n- Touch: invisible left thumbstick, right thumb camera, contextual action, assisted flight.\n\nBring a friend from the menu. Same-device rooms use a short neighborhood code. Direct online play exchanges an invitation and a reply using WebRTC; some networks may prevent direct connections. AI Hub connects its sessions automatically. Both players share progress, can retrieve parts, and can glow to protect their friend.\n\nThe title starts an AI-played attract adventure after ten seconds without input. Any input returns to the menu. A checkpoint is saved locally after finding parts and starting flight.\n\n## Development\n\nAll authored modules and validation artifacts are here. \"'`npm run build` makes a development `index.html` with local dependencies and the distributable HTML in the parent folder. `npm test` runs the unified headless checks; it prints only failures and one count. The test runner owns and closes its OS-selected localhost server and browser.\n\n`?webgl=1` forces the renderer'\"'s WebGL compatibility backend for visual inspection on headless machines whose WebGPU screenshots are blank. Normal play selects WebGPU automatically. \"'`window.__game` exposes state and deterministic gameplay controls for scripted QA.\n\nCredits and the machine-readable `exit-interview.json` beside the final HTML contain the requested source and testing reports.\nEOF'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"agent_message","text":"The demo includes the complete ending, optional flower healing, searchlight stealth, a protective glow, a four-note signal puzzle, and a flight course with twelve rings and search vans. The live developer sliders cover movement, detection, friendship recovery, glow cost, and flight speed; the final checks are now focused on finding integration bugs.\n"}}
